(begin-tx "CONF-06 - the GS-07 default, on a module that is genuinely unregistered")
(namespace "ouronet-ns")
(print "--- [CONF-06 · 01 · P|UR_IMP before any registration] ---")
;;<<CONF-06>> GS-07, WHICH WAS UNPROTECTED IN ALL 61 MODULES UNTIL THIS BLOCK.
;;
;;GS-07 made `P|UR_IMP` default to the module's own SECURE capability guard instead of doing a bare
;;`read`, which RAISES `No value found in table <M>_P|MT for key: InterModulePolicies` when no row
;;exists. Nothing tested it. Proven by reverting 06_VCT.pact's P|UR_IMP to the pre-GS-07 bare read
;;and re-running the whole gate: 86 entrypoints, 21,527 assertions, STILL GREEN. A 61-module safety
;;change that nothing could detect the removal of.
;;
;;WHY THE EXISTING IMC TESTS DO NOT COVER IT. [6.2.10]_AQP-NEGATIVES pins the refusal MESSAGE
;;correctly ("None of the guards passed") -- but by the time it runs, those modules' rows EXIST,
;;because some other module's `P|A_Define` registered with them. The bare read succeeds there. The
;;pre-registration window is the thing GS-07 fixed and the thing nothing entered.
;;
;;FINDING A MODULE THAT IS ACTUALLY UNREGISTERED. Cross-referencing every module holding
;;`P|UR_IMP` (59) against every `ref-X::P|A_AddIMP` target (46) leaves 13 that are NEVER
;;registered with -- and all ten Talos modules are among them (this harness loads Stage 1, so it pins two of them). For those, the GS-07 default is not
;;a nicety, it is the only thing standing between a caller and a raw table error naming a row key.
(let
    (
        (ref-P|TS01C1:module{OuronetPolicyV2} TS01-C1)
        (ref-P|TS01C3:module{OuronetPolicyV2} TS01-C3)
        (ref-P|DPMF:module{OuronetPolicyV2} DPMF)
    )
    (map print
        [
            ;;1] THE READER. An unregistered module answers with a ONE-element policy list -- its own
            ;;   SECURE capability guard -- rather than raising. Reverting GS-07 turns each of these
            ;;   into "No value found in table ..." and the expect dies on the error, not the value.
            (expect "<<CONF-06>> TS01-C1 is unregistered, and P|UR_IMP answers with exactly one guard"
                1 (length (ref-P|TS01C1::P|UR_IMP)))
            (expect "<<CONF-06>> ...same for TS01-C3"
                1 (length (ref-P|TS01C3::P|UR_IMP)))
            ;;DPMF IS DIFFERENT, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS WORTH PINNING. It does not fail the GS-07
            ;;   way -- it fails EARLIER. `with-default-read` defaults a missing ROW; it cannot
            ;;   default a missing TABLE. DPMF `deftable`s P|T and P|MT and then calls
            ;;   `create-table` ZERO times -- every other module creates its tables at the end of
            ;;   the file. So the legacy MetaFungible module is deployed with no storage at all and
            ;;   every storage-backed function in it errors. Combined with the separate finding that
            ;;   NOTHING in 1_SOVEREIGN or 2_CITIZEN calls `ref-DPMF::`, this states the strong form:
            ;;   DPMF is not merely unreferenced, it is INERT. Asserted rather than left implicit, so
            ;;   a half-migration that creates the tables without wiring the callers goes red here.
            (expect-failure "<<CONF-06>> DPMF is INERT -- it never create-tables its policy storage"
                "Table ouronet-ns.DPMF_P|MT not found"
                (ref-P|DPMF::P|UR_IMP))
            ;;2] THE GATE BUILT ON IT, pinned BY MESSAGE. This is the assertion that fails if GS-07
            ;;   is reverted: the refusal must be the POLICY refusal. A table error is also a
            ;;   refusal, and from the outside the two are indistinguishable unless the message is
            ;;   checked -- the same lesson RT-C-001 recorded about a shadowed admin gate.
            (expect-failure "<<CONF-06>> P|UEV_IMC on an unregistered module refuses BY POLICY, not by table error"
                "None of the guards passed"
                (ref-P|TS01C1::P|UEV_IMC))
            (expect-failure "<<CONF-06>> ...and the same on a second, independent Talos module"
                "None of the guards passed"
                (ref-P|TS01C3::P|UEV_IMC))
            ;;3] NON-VACUITY. The guard the default seeds is a capability guard, so it is
            ;;   unsatisfiable from outside the owning module BY CONSTRUCTION -- not merely absent.
            ;;   A default of [] would also make UEV_Any refuse, and would prove nothing about what
            ;;   the list contains; this asserts the list is non-empty AND still refuses.
            (expect "<<CONF-06>> the refusal is a NON-EMPTY guard list, not an empty one"
                true (> (length (ref-P|TS01C1::P|UR_IMP)) 0))
        ])
)
(print "===^^^===")
(commit-tx)
(print "")
(print "=== END modules/CONFORMANCE.repl ===")
